WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.528
California law defines two kinds of plans.

2
00:00:02.528 --> 00:00:09.268
The general plan is the city's long-range constitution for land use, usually looking twenty to thirty years out.

3
00:00:09.268 --> 00:00:13.480
A specific plan is a tighter set of standards for one geographic area.

4
00:00:13.480 --> 00:00:15.105
Zoning is not a third plan.

5
00:00:15.105 --> 00:00:23.711
It is the ordinance that carries the general plan into lot-by-lot rules: uses, height, setbacks, lot coverage, landscaping, signs, and parking.

6
00:00:23.711 --> 00:00:32.196
Vision documents and area studies can guide you, but they are not the standard unless the council has adopted them into the plan or the code.

7
00:00:32.196 --> 00:00:39.302
State law requires general plan elements for land use, circulation, housing, conservation, open space, noise, and safety.

8
00:00:39.302 --> 00:00:44.647
Where a city has disadvantaged communities, it also needs an environmental justice element.

9
00:00:44.647 --> 00:00:51.049
Cities in certain air basins, including the South Coast basin that covers Torrance, also address air quality.

10
00:00:51.049 --> 00:00:56.100
The elements have to agree with each other, and the zoning has to agree with the plan.

11
00:00:56.100 --> 00:01:01.620
A project that needs the zoning changed is asking for a legislative act, not a routine permit.

12
00:01:01.620 --> 00:01:03.676
Inside the plan, read three layers.

13
00:01:03.676 --> 00:01:05.849
A goal is the outcome the city wants.

14
00:01:05.849 --> 00:01:08.903
A policy is the commitment that regulates decisions.

15
00:01:08.903 --> 00:01:13.190
An action, sometimes called an implementation program, is the to-do list.

16
00:01:13.190 --> 00:01:18.476
A hearing that cites a goal and ignores the policy underneath it is arguing with a slogan.

17
00:01:18.476 --> 00:01:22.156
The housing element is the only element the state certifies.

18
00:01:22.156 --> 00:01:26.020
The Department of Housing and Community Development reviews it.

19
00:01:26.020 --> 00:01:27.921
It runs on an eight-year cycle.

20
00:01:27.921 --> 00:01:36.631
It must plan sites, by income category, for the city's share of the regional housing need, and it must name the barriers the city will remove.

21
00:01:36.631 --> 00:01:39.329
Every year the city files a progress report.

22
00:01:39.329 --> 00:01:46.566
If the housing element and the zoning disagree, the housing element can control until the zoning is brought into line.

23
00:01:46.566 --> 00:01:55.950
Torrance's current cycle, 2021 through 2029, requires the city to plan for 4,939 homes, including more than 2,400 for very low and low income households.

24
00:01:55.950 --> 00:02:05.089
The state found the element compliant in 2022 on the condition that sites be rezoned, and later warned that late rezoning could cost that compliance.

25
00:02:05.089 --> 00:02:11.185
Traditional zoning, the kind upheld in Euclid, separates uses and regulates intensity and setbacks.

26
00:02:11.185 --> 00:02:18.573
Form-based rules care more about the shape of the building, how it meets the street, and the design of the public realm.

27
00:02:18.573 --> 00:02:23.499
Torrance still lives mostly in the older system, with important overlays on top.

28
00:02:23.499 --> 00:02:30.149
The general plan adopted in April 2010 organizes the city as a deliberate mix of homes, shops, and industry.

29
00:02:30.149 --> 00:02:41.601
The Hawthorne Boulevard Corridor Specific Plan, adopted in 1996, covers about eight hundred fifty-seven acres in five districts and still requires a use permit for much of its mixed use.

30
00:02:41.601 --> 00:02:48.990
The 2023 Housing Corridor Overlay, about ninety-five acres in seven areas, allows housing by right at specified heights.

31
00:02:48.990 --> 00:02:57.856
Downtown, an overlay on the original Olmsted tract sends most new development to this commission, and a 2017 ordinance protects local landmarks.

32
00:02:57.856 --> 00:03:01.797
When a project arrives, name the layer that actually governs it.

33
00:03:01.797 --> 00:03:03.336
General plan designation.

34
00:03:03.336 --> 00:03:03.767
Zoning.

35
00:03:03.767 --> 00:03:05.553
Any specific plan or overlay.

36
00:03:05.553 --> 00:03:11.525
Then the state housing laws, which can waive local standards even when every local layer says no.

37
00:03:11.525 --> 00:03:16.020
The next episodes are about how to do that reading on a real application.

38
00:03:16.020 --> 00:03:23.963
See the Planning Commission 101 materials from the 2026 academy, and Torrance's adopted general plan, zoning, and specific plans.
